  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE...Localized urban flash flooding possible this morning \nas warm front lifts north.\n\nA stalled frontal boundary over Southern Virginia is lifting north \nacross our area as a warm front. A slug of deep tropical moisture is \nalready surging northward into the Mid-Atlantic, with PWATs analyzed \nat or over 2\" early this morning. Several of the 00Z high res models \nindicate the potential for heavy rain amounts of 1-3\" across the I-\n95 metro area from Fredericksburg up to Washington DC. It will be \nhard for models to show consensus on any given location since \nconvection will be driven by warm advection along the front, and \naided by forcing for ascent from an upper low lover the Mid-South. \nIf any of this heavy rain occurs over the urban sprawl around the \nD.C. metro, poor drainage will support a risk of flash flooding. A \nFlood Watch is in effect through 11 AM this morning for the \npreviously mentioned areas along I-95.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Daily shower and thunderstorm chances are expected \nthrough early next week.\n\nIn the wake of what morning convection materializes, the models do \nshow a brief lull in the action. The northward pushing warm front \neventually stalls nearby before shearing out. Likewise, the upper \nlow across the Mid-South region begins to fill although the \nreflection of this circulation still shows up across southern West \nVirginia. \n\nA deep southerly flow will continue around high pressure over the \nAtlantic, ushering plenty of moisture. PWATS will be near or greater \nthan 2\" in the valleys with 1-2\" in the mountains, which is about 1-\n2 SD above climo. A surface trough and shortwave energy will trigger \nscattered showers/thunderstorms, but it should not be a washout \nduring the afternoon/evening. The main threat will be brief heavy \ndownpours and gusty winds. However, DCAPE and shear are on the lower \nside, so the probability of severe storms is lower. If some storms \ntrain over the same area, localized flash flooding can occur. The \npattern does favor the possibility for training convection (cloud \nlayer winds nearly parallel to low-level wind), but the coverage \nshould be isolated. \n\nA similar pattern will hold on for Thursday and Friday while the \nsubtropical jet strengthens to the north and west. Therefore, more \nshowers and thunderstorms are likely along with the threat for \nisolated instances of flash flooding and locally damaging wind \ngusts. Hotter conditions may cause relatively steeper low-level \nlapse rates and the wind field strengthens a bit (especially \nFriday). \n\nA quasi-zonal flow aloft will develop for the weekend into next \nweek, bringing more seasonably hot and humid conditions along with \ndaily chances for showers and thunderstorms.",
